          </h2>

          <p>
            Gas can spouts changed in the late 2000s because of emissions rules, and that explains why so many cans are frustrating to pour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Modern Cans Are Different</h3>

          <p>
            EPA and California Air Resources Board rules require consumer fuel containers to limit vapor emissions and spills. That is why old-style cans with separate vent caps disappeared and spouts became self-closing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Compliance Looks Like</h3>

          <p>
            Compliant listings say so: the Scepter and Midwest list EPA/CARB compliance, and the Wavian gives its CARB executive order number. In CARB states, buy a can that states compliance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Steel Safety Cans Fit</h3>

          <p>
            Eagle and Justrite list OSHA and NFPA workplace standards rather than EPA/CARB consumer rules. They are excellent for workshop storage, but if you are in a CARB state, check how local rules treat them.
          </p>
        </div>

