          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Size Gas Can Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the can to what you are filling and how much you need to store.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1 to 2.5 Gallons</h3>

          <p>
            Small cans, like the Midwest 2-gallon and No-Spill 2.5-gallon, suit trimmers, blowers and push mowers. They are light to pour and ideal for 2-cycle mix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5 Gallons</h3>

          <p>
            Five gallons is the standard for generators, riding mowers and ATVs. A full 5-gallon can weighs over 30 pounds, so a good handle or a rotating spout like the SureCan's helps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much to Store for Outages</h3>

          <p>
            Estimate your generator's fuel use per day, multiply by the days you want to cover, and keep that amount in several cans rather than one large one. Our guide on <a href="/how-long-can-a-generator-run-continuously/">how long a generator can run</a> helps estimate it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Storing Gas Safely at Home
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gasoline is safe to store when you follow a few rules.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Keep It</h3>

          <p>
            Store cans in a detached shed or garage, away from living spaces, out of direct sun and well away from water heaters, furnaces or anything with a pilot light or spark. Keep them off concrete if your can maker advises it and out of reach of children.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Long Gas Lasts</h3>

          <p>
            Untreated gasoline starts to degrade within a few months. Add stabilizer when you fill the can and rotate stored fuel; see our <a href="/best-fuel-stabilizer/">fuel stabilizer guide</a> for products and dosing. Keep a spare quart of the right <a href="/best-generator-oil/">generator oil</a> on the same shelf, because a generator that runs for days in an outage will need an oil change partway through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Local Limits</h3>

          <p>
            Many local fire codes limit how much gasoline you can store at home. Check before stockpiling for outages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="generators" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas Cans for Generators and Power Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            A generator is only as useful as the fuel you have ready for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fill Before the Storm</h3>

          <p>
            Gas stations often lose power or run dry during widespread outages. Keep treated fuel on hand and rotate it into your car every few months so it stays fresh.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refill Safely</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the generator off and let it cool before refueling; spilling gas on a hot engine is a fire risk. A controllable spout, like the Scepter's, makes topping up a small tank easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose the Right Generator</h3>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator guide</a> and <a href="/best-dual-fuel-inverter-generator/">dual fuel inverter generator guide</a> cover models that can also run on propane, which stores indefinitely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pouring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Pour From a Modern Gas Can Without Spilling
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most spills come from fighting the spout. A little technique helps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Set the Can Down First</h3>

          <p>
            Rest the can on a stable surface higher than the tank when you can, rather than holding its full weight in the air.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Engage the Spout Fully</h3>

          <p>
            Seat the spout in the fill opening before pressing the button or trigger. Self-closing spouts only flow when fully engaged.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Vent</h3>

          <p>
            Cans with vented spouts, like the Scepter, flow faster. With others, pour steadily and let air glug in rather than squeezing the can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Stop Short of Full</h3>

          <p>
            Leave room in the tank for expansion. Auto shut-off spouts, like the Midwest's, do this for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-cycle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Keeping 2-Cycle Mix and Diesel Separate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mixing up fuels is an expensive mistake that good can habits prevent.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use Color Codes</h3>

          <p>
            Red is the standard color for gasoline, yellow for diesel and blue for kerosene. The Scepter is sold in red for gasoline and yellow for diesel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Dedicated Can for Mix</h3>

          <p>
            Keep a separate small can, like the No-Spill 2.5-gallon or Midwest 2-gallon, only for 2-cycle mix, and label it. Mixed fuel in a 4-cycle engine causes smoking and fouled plugs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Label Everything</h3>

          <p>
            Write the fuel type, mix ratio and fill date on each can with a marker. It makes rotation and safety easier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replacement-spouts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Replacement Spouts and Old Cans
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many people search for replacement spouts because an old can's spout broke or a new one pours too slowly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Replacement Makes Sense</h3>

          <p>
            If the can body is sound, a maker's own replacement spout restores it. Scepter and Midwest both sell replacement spouts for their cans.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Replace the Can</h3>

          <p>
            Plastic cans that are cracked, brittle, swollen or leaking should be replaced, not repaired. Old cans without vapor seals also let fuel go stale faster.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Avoid Mismatched Parts</h3>

          <p>
            Spouts and caps are designed for specific cans and threads. Mismatched parts can leak, so buy the spout made for your can.
          </p>
        </div>

