          <p>
            Trimmer and blower tanks have small openings. The No-Spill's 7/8-inch spout and thumb button, or the Midwest's auto shut-off spout, put fuel in without overfilling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Debris Out</h3>

          <p>
            A speck of grit can block a small carburetor jet. The No-Spill's built-in 20-mesh screen and dust cover keep debris out of the fuel you pour.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Can to the Season</h3>

          <p>
            A 2- or 2.5-gallon can of treated fuel is usually enough for a season of trimming and blowing. Our guides to the <a href="/best-gas-string-trimmer/">best gas string trimmers</a> and <a href="/best-gas-leaf-blower/">best gas leaf blowers</a> cover the engines themselves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transfer-pumps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Funnels and Transfer Pumps
          </h2>

          <p>
            Accessories can make pouring easier, especially with heavy cans.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Funnels</h3>

          <p>
            The Scepter includes a funnel with a storage ring, which speeds up filling and catches drips. A dedicated fuel funnel with a screen works with any can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transfer Pumps</h3>

          <p>
            Battery or siphon transfer pumps move fuel from a can to a tank without lifting. They help when refilling a large generator or tractor tank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep Accessories Clean</h3>

          <p>
            Store funnels and pump hoses capped or bagged so dirt does not get into your fuel. Use separate accessories for diesel and gasoline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vehicles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Carrying Gas Cans in a Car or Truck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Transporting fuel safely takes a little care.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Secure It Upright</h3>

          <p>
            Strap the can upright in a truck bed or trailer so it cannot tip or slide. Avoid carrying fuel inside the passenger area of a car where possible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fill It on the Ground</h3>

          <p>
            At the pump, place the can on the ground before filling, not in a truck bed or trunk, to reduce the risk of static discharge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vehicle-Mounted Cans</h3>

          <p>
            Steel jerry cans like the Wavian fit standard can mounts for off-road vehicles and trailers, which keeps them secure on rough roads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="disposal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Disposing of Old Gas and Old Cans
          </h2>

          <p>
            Old fuel and worn-out cans need proper disposal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old Gasoline</h3>

          <p>
            Stale gas that is still clear can often be mixed in small amounts into a car's full tank. Gas that is dark, separated or full of debris should go to a household hazardous waste collection site.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Pour It Out</h3>

          <p>
            Never pour gasoline on the ground, down a drain or into the trash. It contaminates soil and water and is a fire hazard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old Cans</h3>

          <p>
            Empty cans still contain vapor. Ask your local waste service how to dispose of them, and replace any can that is cracked, brittle or leaking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-many-cans" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Several Small Cans or One Big One?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For outage planning, how you split your fuel matters as much as how much you keep.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Several 5-Gallon Cans Beat One Large Container</h3>

          <p>
            A full 5-gallon can already weighs over 30 pounds. Larger containers are hard to lift and pour into a generator, and if one leaks or gets contaminated you lose everything in it. Several 5-gallon cans spread the risk and are easier to rotate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotate on a Schedule</h3>

          <p>
            Number your cans and use the oldest first. Pour it into your car's tank, refill with fresh gas and stabilizer, and put it at the back of the line. That way no can sits for more than a few months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep One Small Can Separate</h3>

          <p>
            A dedicated 2-gallon can for small engines and 2-cycle mix, like the Midwest or No-Spill, keeps your generator cans full and ready while you mow and trim.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Them Before Storm Season</h3>

          <p>
            Before outage season, check each can for cracks, a working spout and a tight cap, and top up anything that has been used. A can that leaks or will not pour is no help when the power is out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            15. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Gas Near a Pilot Light</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline vapor is heavier than air and travels along the floor. Keep cans away from water heaters, furnaces and anything with a flame or spark, ideally in a detached shed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refueling a Hot Generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spilled gas on a hot engine can ignite. Turn the generator off and let it cool before refilling, even in the middle of an outage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Untreated Gas in Storage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas starts to go stale within months. Add stabilizer at the pump and rotate stored fuel, or your outage fuel may not start the generator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Up Fuel Types</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Using one can for both gasoline and 2-cycle mix, or gasoline and diesel, eventually puts the wrong fuel in an engine. Use color-coded, labeled cans.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Compliance in a CARB State</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In California and other CARB states, consumer fuel containers must be compliant. Choose a can that states EPA/CARB compliance or lists a CARB executive order number.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

