            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas can?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Scepter 5-gallon SmartControl is the best gas can. Its listing states EPA/CARB compliance, a flame mitigation device and a controllable spout that pours up to 3 gallons per minute with the included funnel. For workshop storage, the Eagle Type I steel safety can is the sturdiest choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best 5 gallon gas can?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Scepter SmartControl for everyday use, the Eagle or Justrite Type I steel cans for long-term storage, and the SureCan if you want a rotating spout that pours without lifting and tipping. Check compliance if you live in a CARB state.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are metal gas cans better than plastic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Metal safety cans are tougher and include flame arresters and self-closing, pressure-relieving lids, which makes them better for storing fuel in a garage or shop. Plastic cans are lighter, cheaper and easier for everyday refilling. Many homes keep one of each.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gas can for long-term storage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A steel safety can like the Eagle UI50S or Justrite Type I, or a steel jerry can like the Wavian. Store it in a cool, detached space, add fuel stabilizer when you fill it, and rotate the fuel every few months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are new gas cans so hard to pour?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                EPA and CARB rules require spouts that seal automatically to limit vapor and spills, which slowed many designs. Better cans fix it with vented spouts and controllable flow, like the Scepter SmartControl, or auto shut-off spouts like the Midwest Quick-Flow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can gas be stored in a gas can?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Untreated gasoline begins to degrade within a few months. With fuel stabilizer added when the can is filled, it lasts considerably longer. Rotate stored fuel into your car regularly and refill with fresh, treated gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What color gas can is for diesel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yellow is the standard color for diesel, red for gasoline and blue for kerosene. Using the right color helps prevent putting the wrong fuel in an engine. Scepter sells its SmartControl can in red for gasoline and yellow for diesel.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
